---
/**
 * Ridges — the terrain the camera travels across.
 *
 * Three bands by default, two below 640px: three reads as clutter on a phone
 * (04-experience.md §7).
 *
 * **Option C — the scroll-driven camera (D-044).** Each band pans horizontally
 * as the page scrolls, at a rate set by its depth: the far hills barely move,
 * the near ones travel four times as far. Vertical scrolling therefore reads as
 * lateral travel through a landscape rather than as a page moving over a
 * backdrop. This supersedes the "no parallax, no scroll listener" half of
 * D-027.
 *
 * There is still no scroll listener. `animation-timeline: scroll()` is native
 * CSS: the browser drives the animation from scroll position off the main
 * thread, so the page needs no JavaScript for any of this and `nojs.spec.ts`
 * is untouched. Browsers without support fall through the `@supports` guard to
 * the static composition, which is exactly the pre-camera design.
 *
 * The paths span -600 → 1800 in a 0 → 1200 viewBox: 600 user units of terrain
 * hidden off each edge, so panning never exposes a hard edge. The furthest a
 * band travels is 300, half the margin.
 */
---

<svg
  class="ridges"
  aria-hidden="true"
  focusable="false"
  viewBox="0 0 1200 300"
  preserveAspectRatio="none"
>
  <path class="r-far"  d="M-600 150 q150-58 300-30 q170 32 330-22 q160-54 320-8 q140 40 250 12 q150-50 300-26 q170 34 330-18 q160-50 320-10 q140 42 250 14 v210 H-600 Z" />
  <path class="r-mid"  d="M-600 202 q180-46 340-16 q180 34 340-18 q170-56 330-4 q110 36 190 14 q180-44 340-14 q180 32 340-16 q170-54 330-6 q110 34 190 12 v190 H-600 Z" />
  <path class="r-near" d="M-600 250 q200-38 380-10 q200 30 400-16 q200-46 420 6 q200-36 380-12 q200 28 400-14 q200-44 420 8 v150 H-600 Z" />
</svg>

<style>
  .ridges {
    position: fixed;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    z-index: -2;
    width: 100%;
    height: 34vh;
    min-height: 190px;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .r-far  { fill: var(--ridge-far); }
  .r-mid  { fill: var(--ridge-mid); }
  .r-near { fill: var(--ridge-near); }

  /* The camera. Rates are the whole illusion: if they were equal the terrain
     would slide as one flat sheet and read as a bug rather than as depth. */
  @supports (animation-timeline: scroll()) {
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
      .r-far, .r-mid, .r-near {
        animation-timeline: scroll(root block);
        animation-timing-function: linear;
        animation-fill-mode: both;
      }
      .r-far  { animation-name: pan-far; }
      .r-mid  { animation-name: pan-mid; }
      .r-near { animation-name: pan-near; }
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .r-far { display: none; }
    .ridges { height: 26vh; min-height: 150px; }
  }
</style>
